---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-asthma`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Asthma',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Asthma', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'J45', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Asthma', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Asthma: Exacerbations, Steroid Use, and the VIDA Trial"
  description="Does vitamin D reduce asthma attacks? Cochrane meta-analysis of ~1,100 patients, VIDA trial, VDAART pregnancy trial results and dosing guidance."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-asthma", label: "Vitamin D & Asthma" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Asthma</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vitamin D deficiency is more common in adults and children with asthma than in
      controls, and it correlates with worse lung function and more frequent exacerbations
      in observational cohorts. Trial evidence for supplementation is mixed but positive
      overall for reducing severe attacks in deficient patients.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The Cochrane evidence</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A 2016 Cochrane systematic review (nine trials, ~1,100 patients) concluded vitamin D
      supplementation reduced the rate of asthma exacerbations requiring systemic
      corticosteroids by roughly 30%, and cut ED-visit or hospitalisation-triggering
      attacks. The 2023 update found the effect largely confined to adults with baseline
      deficiency (25(OH)D &lt; 25 nmol/L / &lt; 10 ng/mL).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Key individual trials</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>VIDA (2014)</strong> — 408 adults with mild-moderate asthma and low vitamin D. 100,000 IU bolus + 4,000 IU/day did not improve time-to-treatment-failure primary endpoint, but reduced overall exacerbation rate.</li>
      <li><strong>VDAART (2016)</strong> — maternal 4,400 IU/day in pregnancy vs 400 IU/day; 20% relative reduction in offspring asthma/recurrent wheeze by age 3, though not statistically significant at the pre-specified threshold.</li>
      <li><strong>Copenhagen COPSAC (2016)</strong> — similar pregnancy design; 24% reduction in persistent wheeze by age 3.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Mechanisms</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Vitamin D modulates T-helper cell balance (favouring Treg over Th2/Th17 responses),
      induces the antimicrobial peptide cathelicidin (which may reduce viral triggers of
      asthma), and influences airway smooth muscle proliferation. Deficiency is associated
      with steroid resistance in some asthma phenotypes.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical guidance</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Test 25(OH)D in adults with poorly controlled asthma.</li>
      <li>If deficient (&lt; 20 ng/mL), correct with 2,000–4,000 IU/day for 8–12 weeks, then re-test.</li>
      <li>If already sufficient, adding more is unlikely to reduce exacerbations.</li>
      <li>Vitamin D supplementation is an adjunct to, not a replacement for, controller inhalers.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Do not adjust asthma medications based on
      vitamin D status without your prescriber's input.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-asthma" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
